 "author_id_missing": false,
 "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1410226/is-there-any-use-for-c-throw-decoration",
 "body_html": "<p>I've started using C++ exceptions in a uniform manner, and now I'd like the compiler (g++) to check that there are no \"exception leaks\". The <code>throw</code> decoration should do this, like <code>const</code> does for constness of class methods.</p>\n\n<p>Well, it doesn't.</p>\n\n<p>Using <code>throw</code> is still documentary, but may even be dangerously misleading if others think a function cannot throw other exceptions than those listed in its documentation.</p>\n\n<p>Can g++ somehow be persuaded to be more strict on its throw-checking, i.e. really making sure a function decorated as <code>throw()</code> will never-ever throw anything.</p>\n\n<p>Edit:\nFound <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/88573/should-i-use-an-exception-specifier-in-c\">this question</a> handling the subject widely.</p>\n",
 "passes": [
  "offcwe"
 ],
 "data_source": "so_offcwe (refetch with full paging)",
 "in_main_retained_480": false,
 "in_main_nonNA_coded_346": false,
 "in_offcwe_nonNA_coded": false,
 "events": [
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 1410239,
   "parent_id": 1410226,
   "author_user_id": 47351,
   "author_display_name": "RvdK",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/1410239",
   "score": 0,
   "created_utc": "2009-09-11T10:51:03Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2009-09-11T10:51:03Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>I dont know if the checker/compiler can do that. Perhaps it's just easier to comment it in the function declaration. Well it aint fool proof but if people see that there can be an exception thrown than most of the people will wrap the function call in try and catch.</p>\n\n<pre><code>//Can throw Exception\nGetFoo();\n</code></pre>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 1410255,
   "parent_id": 1410226,
   "author_user_id": 96963,
   "author_display_name": "Kim Gräsman",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/1410255",
   "score": 17,
   "created_utc": "2009-09-11T10:56:38Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2009-09-11T10:56:38Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>It doesn't check compile-time, but a conforming compiler should ensure it at run-time.</p>\n\n<p>If a function throws anything outside of its throw-declaration, the C++ run-time should call std::unexpected, if I recall correctly.</p>\n"
  },
